  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1. Frost concerns for portions of the area later tonight \ninto early Monday morning.\n\nA narrow mid level ridge from the upper Ohio Valley to southern New \nEngland is forecast to weaken some tonight into Monday. Surface high \npressure stretched southwest to northeast across our area will also \nweaken a bit through tonight. As this occurs, a closed low off the \ncoast of the Carolinas will support surface low pressure also well \noff those coasts. This will start to tighten the pressure gradient \nnorthward into our area later tonight and Monday morning. This will \nhave implications on the surface winds. Most inland areas should \ndecouple for a while tonight, however areas closer to the coast \n(especially near and south/east of I-95) may have a northeasterly \nwind increase some overnight and especially Monday morning. Given \nthe presence of the ridge aloft and surface high pressure directly \nover the interior areas longer, there is a greater risk of frost \ntonight and early Monday morning. Therefore, opted to issue a Frost \nAdvisory for areas near and north/west of I-95 for where the growing \nseason continues. For areas south and east of there, patchy frost is \npossible away from the coasts but with lower confidence on \nwidespread frost opted to hold off on any frost advisories. \n\nAfter a chilly/cold start Monday, a milder afternoon is forecast \nwith high temperatures topping out right near average away from the \nimmediate coast. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A nor'easter developing off the coast of the\nsouthern Mid-Atlantic is expected to bring coastal impacts and \ncreate hazardous marine conditions through at least the middle \nof this week.\n\nThe base of a deep trough axis that is located just offshore\nwill cutoff into an upper level low tonight which will then meander\nover the southern Mid- Atlantic coast over the next few days. \nAt the same time, strong and expansive high pressure will be \nbuilding over the Ohio Valley. As a result, a nor'easter is \ndeveloping off the North Carolina coast and is expected to \ncontinue to strengthen and meander offshore into the middle of\nthis week.\n\nOverall, confidence has increased that through at least\nWednesday, the nor'easter will remain close enough to bring\nmultiple impacts to our coastal areas. Persistently strong \nnortheast winds will push water towards the coast. Multiple \nrounds of coastal flooding are likely (more details to come). \nIncreasing seas may result in beach erosion in some areas as\nwell. For areas along the immediate coast, gusty winds up to\naround 30-35 mph are possible.\n\nInland, impacts will be limited to none with how far offshore\nthe storm is likely to be. In fact, outside of a few showers at\nthe coast, little to no precipitation is likely at all over \nland areas.\n\nFor Thursday and beyond, there still remains more uncertainty in\nthe forecast. However, the prevailing consensus is trending \ntowards the storm being swept out to sea which would likely \nresult in an improvement in conditions along the coast. Notably,\nthough, there are still multiple members of both the EC and \nGEFS ensemble suites that have the nor'easter continue to linger\nin our offshore waters towards the end of the week.",
